<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mexico City</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> City is the capital of Mexico, built on the ruins of the Aztec capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>. After the Spanish conquest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> deliberately razed the Aztec city and founded the new colonial capital on its site. It became the seat of the viceroyalty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mexico-city.jpg" alt="Mexico City" width="320" height="168" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gobierno CDMX · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sobrevuelos_CDMX_HJ2A4913_(25514321687)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tenochtitlan, one of the largest and most sophisticated cities on earth in 1519, was razed and <b>Mexico City</b> built on its ruins, an act of symbolic obliteration matched by the systematic destruction of Aztec religious and historical manuscripts by Spanish clergy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Despite a celebrated Mexican victory at the Battle of Puebla on 5 May 1862, French troops took <b>Mexico City</b> in 1863, and in 1864 Archduke Maximilian of Austria accepted the Mexican crown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Cortés named the conquered lands New Spain and had <b>Mexico City</b> built on the ruins of the Aztec capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Chapultepec Peace Accords, signed in <b>Mexico City</b> on 16 January 1992, the army was reduced and removed from internal security, the old security forces were abolished, a National Civil Police was created and the FMLN became a legal party.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>The British and French ministers hurried to <b>Mexico City</b>, where Mexico signed a &quot;Diplomatic Act&quot; offering to recognise an independent Texas with mediated boundaries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-texas">Republic of Texas · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1562 the Franciscan friar Diego de Landa burned the Maya books he found at Maní in Yucatán as works of the devil; only four Maya books survive today, in Dresden, Madrid, Paris and <b>Mexico City</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion">Mesoamerican religion · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
